  "full_response": "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is an approach that improves the performance Large Language Models (LLMs) by enhancing their ability to retrieve information from a knowledge base to answer user queries more accurately and contextually.\n\nThe improvements in LLM accuracy come from the combination of LLMs' inherent text-generation capabilities with the retrieval of relevant context-specific facts from a structured data store. Here's how RAG improves LLM accuracy:\n\n1. Contextualization: By retrieving relevant context-specific facts from knowledge base, LLMs can generate more accurate and contextually relevant responses.\n2. Fact-checking: The retrieved information enables LLMs to cross-verify the information they generate, thus reducing the probability of spreading misinformation or generating incorrect facts. This promotes the generation of reliable and credible answers.\n3. Flexibility: RAG has the flexibility to work with a variety of knowledge bases, such as databases, enterprise knowledge graphs, or tables, making it adaptable to different use-cases and applications.\n4. Efficiency: By specifically retrieving the required data, RAG narrows down the search space for generation, thus eliminating the need to pre-train or fine-tune the model on vast amounts of domain-specific data. This reduces the training time and the need for costly retraining efforts.\n\nOverall, RAG presents a powerful approach that capitalizes on the strengths of both LLMs and retrieval-intensive knowledge bases, resulting in a more accurate, reliable, and flexible generation of information to answer user queries.",
